<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Waterford</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> is a county in the province of Munster, in the south-east of Ireland, named after the city of Waterford, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> in the early 10th century. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its lands belonged to the Déisi, and the monastery of Lismore was one of Ireland's great schools. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Normans</a> took Waterford city in 1170, beginning the English conquest of Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/county-waterford.jpg" alt="County Waterford" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mike Searle] · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castles_of_Munster,_Lismore,_Waterford-geograph-3038250.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>County <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> lies in the province of Munster and is named after the city of Waterford. It has two mountain ranges, the Knockmealdown Mountains and the Comeragh Mountains, and Knockmealdown, at 794 m, is its highest point. It is crossed by many rivers, among them the Suir, 184 km long, and the Munster Blackwater, 168 km long. Its volcanic coastline has over thirty beaches, and a large stretch of it, known as the Copper Coast, is designated a UNESCO Geopark for its geological importance. The county has eight historic baronies, the westernmost being Decies-within-Drum and Decies-without-Drum, which are separated by the Drum-Fineen hills.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The county is based on the historic Gaelic territory of the Déise, and is still colloquially called the Déise, anglicised as Decies. The name comes from the Déisi, an Irish tribe who, some time between the 4th and 8th centuries, were driven from southern County Meath and north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a>, and moved into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> region, conquering and settling there. Their ancient territory was roughly coextensive with the present Roman Catholic Diocese of Waterford and Lismore, and so included part of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-tipperary">County Tipperary</a>.</p><p>Megalithic tombs and ogham stones are found throughout the county. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> influence survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginalds-tower">Reginald's Tower</a>, one of the first buildings in Ireland to use brick and mortar construction. At Woodstown, 5.5 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> city, a settlement of the 9th century has been discovered. It was the largest Viking settlement found outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and the only large-scale 9th-century Viking settlement discovered to date in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. Other architectural features of the county are products of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland">Anglo-Norman invasion of Ireland</a> and its effects.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The population of the county, including the city, was 127,363 at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Since 2014 Waterford City and County Council has governed both the city and the county. To the west of Dungarvan lies Gaeltacht na nDéise, an Irish-speaking region that includes An Rinn and An Sean Phobal, where the language is still spoken daily.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “County Waterford” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Munster they settled in what is now <b>County Waterford</b> and south Tipperary.</blockquote><a href="/state/deisi-muman">Deisi Muman</a></li></ul></section>
